Blog Post – Linda M. Harrison’s Story

“How Small Health Habits Changed My Life”
by Linda M. Harrison

I never really thought much about my health until I actually got really sick. For most of my life, I was the kind of person who would just ignore symptoms and try to push through everything. If I had a cough or felt tired, I would still go out, still do what I had to do, and not think twice about it.

A few years ago, that mindset caught up to me.

I started feeling really weak and had a bad cough that just wouldn’t go away. At first, I told myself it was nothing serious, probably just a cold. But days went by, and instead of getting better, I felt worse. I remember waking up one morning feeling completely drained, like I had no energy at all. That’s when I realized I couldn’t keep ignoring it.

What made things harder was that I didn’t take the right precautions early on. I was still going out, still being around people, and looking back, I regret that because I could’ve easily passed it to others. That part really stuck with me after everything.

During that time, I came across this website while searching for ways to deal with what I was going through. At first, I just read through some of the stories from other people, and honestly, that helped more than I expected. Seeing that others had gone through similar things made me feel less alone.

One thing that stood out to me was how many people talked about resting, staying home, and actually listening to their body. It sounds simple, but I realized I had been doing the opposite for years.

So I started changing small things:

I stayed home instead of forcing myself to go out
I made sure to rest even when I felt guilty about it
I paid more attention to hygiene, like washing my hands more often
I avoided close contact with others until I felt better

None of these things seemed like a big deal at first, but over time, they made a huge difference. I slowly started to recover, and more importantly, I started to understand how my actions affect other people too.

What this experience taught me is that health isn’t just about you. It’s about everyone around you. The choices you make when you’re sick can either help protect others or put them at risk.

This website didn’t “cure” me, but it helped me realize what I needed to do. It gave me awareness and reminded me that small actions actually matter more than we think.

Now, I take my health more seriously than I ever did before. And if there’s one thing I would tell others, it’s this:

Don’t wait until things get worse to start taking care of yourself.
